

 
How Society Views a Girl-Boy 
Friendship 
 
 
There’s something that’s been living in my head and heart for a long time. It’s not a rant, it’s 
not a complaint, rather it’s something I’ve observed, lived through, and grown up around: 
how society views a friendship between a boy and a girl. 
Let me say it honestly, I’ve had that bond. I have that bond. And I’m proud of it. 
But society? It doesn’t make it easy. 
The moment a guy and a girl are seen together a bit more than usual, people start watching. 
Not in appreciation. But in judgment. Every smile is misunderstood. Every conversation is 
assumed to be flirting. And every shared laugh becomes a rumor. 
What I’ve experienced is a real connection. We vibe. We talk. We support. We understand 
each other without having to explain every little detail. It’s not romantic. It’s not forced. It’s not 
some shallow gossip-worthy thing. It’s real. It’s emotional. It’s pure. But that’s exactly what 
society doesn’t get. 
They think, "There’s no way a boy and girl can be just friends." 
But why? Why is that so hard to accept? 
Some of the deepest conversations I’ve had have been with her. Some of the most honest 
moments of my life – the ones without masks, without ego happened while being around her, 
chatting about anything and everything. That’s not a crush. That’s a comfort zone. That’s not 
attraction. That’s alignment. 
People tried to break it. They’ve said things to me. Tried to make me  believe the other 
person isn’t good. Told me we’re being judged. Tried to turn something sacred into 
something scandalous. I don’t wanna lie saying that it didn’t affect me. For a certain time 
period, I even doubted if this friendship was one-sided? Am I the only one holding on? 
This isn’t one-sided. This isn’t temporary. 
This is rare. 
And in a world full of temporary attachments and confused feelings, rare should be 
celebrated. 
School , WhatsApp, Instagram, and random conversations became an environment where 
others thought they had the right to define our friendship. "They’re definitely more than 
friends," they say. "No way they’re just talking this much for no reason." But what if we are? 
What if we’re simply two humans who found a safe space in each other? 

 
The bond we share isn’t built on compliments, dates, or flirtatious chats. It’s built on listening. 
On mutual understanding. On inside jokes. On checking up after a long, tiring day. In the 
respect that isn’t loud, but consistent. 
She has stood up for me when I didn’t even know I needed it. She noticed changes in my 
behavior even when I stayed silent. That’s not drama. That’s depth. 
People often say, "When the devil sees you winning, he sends a girl into your life." I 
completely disagree. 
Life didn’t send her to distract me. It sent her to remind me who I am. 
She didn’t pull me away from my goals. She gave me calm during the storms. She didn’t 
confuse me. She gave me clarity. 
I’ve had past friendships – some close, some that faded, some that were complicated. But 
this one? It’s not born out of history. It’s born out of understanding. 
No need to name the past. Those chapters had their purpose. They taught me things. But 
this current bond? It’s what showed me what healthy truly feels like. 
It made me realize that not every strong connection has to end in love. Some end up 
becoming the strongest pillar of support in your life. And when that happens, society doesn’t 
know how to process it. 
It labels. It questions. It mocks. 
But here's the truth: we don't owe anyone an explanation. 
Yes, a boy and girl can be friends. Yes, they can talk daily, laugh like crazy, support each 
other without catching feelings. Yes, they can protect each other without possessing each 
other. 
And no – you don’t get to define what this bond means. We do. 
You don’t get to decide if it’s real. We already know it is. 
You don’t get to whisper doubts into our bond. We’re too loud with our understanding for 
your noise to shake us. 
I used to wonder if this bond was one-sided. If maybe I was the one who overvalued it. But 
every word she’s said to me, every gesture she’s made, every time she chose to stay and 
understand – proved otherwise. 
Friendship like this doesn’t need daily validation. It survives on little things: like a shared reel, 
a random thoughtful message, simple presence. 
To the boys out there who are scared of being misunderstood for having a girl as your 
closest friend – don’t be. It’s not your job to convince the world. It’s your job to value what 
you have. 

 
To the girls who are scared of being labeled for having a guy best friend, know this: real 
bonds always survive noise. And your peace is louder than society’s confusion. 
There are very few things in this world as pure as a bond built without expectations, without 
pressure, without judgment. 
If you have it, protect it. If you lost it, appreciate the memories. And if you’ve found it recently 
don’t let anyone question its worth. 
This is my truth. Raw and personal. Based on experience, not imagination. Let the world 
assume. We know the reality. And that’s all that matters. 
This kind of friendship teaches you things no textbook ever will. It teaches you respect  for 
boundaries, for emotions, for silence. It teaches you communication which is not loud, not 
frequent, but meaningful. It teaches you presence – the kind where you don’t need to always 
talk to feel connected. 
And most importantly, it teaches you how to protect someone without controlling them. 
Not every close bond must turn into something romantic. Some are meant to remain the 
strongest non-romantic connections of your life. And that’s powerful too. 
 
 
~Aditya Aryaܷ 
 
 
 
 